  "query_preview": "..\nCurrently there are weak showers east of Tucson with a few\nlightning strikes here and there. This activity is expected to\ncontinue over the next couple of hours. These showers can\nproduce some light precipitation, but will stay less than 0.10\ninches. \n\nLooking at today, the uptick in thunderstorm activity for areas\nsouth and east of Tucson remains on track. The high pressure\nsystem will stay to the north of the area, allowing for SW\nsteering flow at 20-25 kts that will keep the storms moving, and\nintroduce just enough wind shear that storms can be longer lived.\nThe 01/00Z HREF has continued to highlight a 10 percent contour\nof 50kts within a 40 km radius for these areas. The strong \noutflow winds can loft areas of blowing dust, especially in dust\nprone areas along I-10 from San Simon to Willcox. With this, \nthe SPC continues to highlight these areas for marginal risk for\nsevere thunderstorms this afternoon and evening. Outside of the\nwind risk, any storm can produce locally heavy rainfall with an\nisolated risk for flash flooding. \n\nSunday into next week remains to look like an elevated pattern,\nwith easterly flow allowing for continuous moisture and enough\nmotion for the atmosphere to reset each day. With the \ncontinuous moisture flow, as mentioned in the previous \ndiscussion there eventually will be a shift from a wind threat \nto more of a flash flood threat, but still too soon to pinpoint \nwhen that will be.\n\nOutside of storms, the heat remains in place through this\nweekend into early next week with temperatures 6 to 15 degrees\nabove normal. Continue to practice heat safety over the coming\ndays. The area begins to 'cool' to 3 to 6 degrees above normal\nTuesday onward.",
